<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alessandro Farnese, Duke of Parma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1545">1545</a>–<a href="/years/1592">1592</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iii">Alessandro Farnese</a>, Duke of Parma, was the most famous general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a>. As governor of the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> from 1578, he reconquered the southern provinces through diplomacy and siege, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> in 1585. He was due to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-armada">Spanish Armada</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1588. He succeeded his father as duke of Parma and Piacenza in 1586.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alessandro-farnese-duke-of-parma.jpg" alt="Alessandro Farnese, Duke of Parma" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Otto van Veen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vaenius_-_Alexander_Farnese.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iii">Alessandro Farnese</a> was born on 27 August 1545, the son of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottavio-farnese">Ottavio Farnese</a> of Parma and Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, an illegitimate daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. His twin brother Carlo died in 1549. When Margaret became Governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, he went with her to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in 1556 and was delivered to Philip II to ensure the loyalty of the Farnese. He was raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> with his cousin Don Carlos and his half-uncle Don Juan. In 1565 he married Maria of Portugal, and in 1571 he commanded three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Farnese joined Don Juan in the autumn of 1577, and his strategy decided the Battle of Gembloux in early 1578. The sack of Zichem that year, where the garrison was put to the sword, is considered the biggest stain on his career. After Don Juan died in October 1578 he insisted on holding both the posts of Captain-General and Governor-General. By the Treaty of Arras in January 1579 he won over the Catholic nobles of the south, and the northern provinces answered with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-utrecht">Union of Utrecht</a>.</p><p>His troops took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a> after a siege that began on 12 March 1579 and sacked it for three days. In most later sieges he offered generous terms, with no massacres or looting and a gradual return to the Catholic Church.</p><p>The siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> began in 1584, and he cut off the city from the sea by building a bridge of boats across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a> from Kallo to Oordam. Protestants had to leave within four years. He was to lead the land invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-armada">Spanish Armada</a> in 1588, but his barges, blockaded by English ships, could not leave Nieuwpoort and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkirk">Dunkirk</a>. He then entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> twice, relieving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-league">Catholic League</a> in 1590 and marching on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> in 1592, where a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a> shot wounded his forearm at Caudebec.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Spanish courtiers jealous of his success had worked to discredit him with Philip II, who drafted a letter recalling him from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. He knew of his failing health and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> on 11 November 1592 for a final expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, but died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a> at the age of 47. He was buried in the Capuchin church in Parma, and his remains were later moved to the crypt of the Basilica of the Madonna della Steccata. When they were exhumed in January 2020, his death was ascribed to pneumonia. Edward Shepherd Creasy and John Keegan praised his military skill, and his campaigns gave the Spanish crown lasting control of the southern provinces.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alexander Farnese, Duke of Parma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the Habsburgs Brabant became a main theatre of the Eighty Years' War, with the Spanish Fury at Antwerp in 1576, <b>Alexander Farnese</b>'s siege of Antwerp in 1584–1585, and in 1706 the Duke of Marlborough's victory at the Battle of Ramillies gave the allies control of the duchy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=1575" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alessandro Farnese, Duke of Parma"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=1575">What was here in 1575? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
